---

title: Turtle Bay Style Vegan Motherclucker Burger
tags:
  - vegan
  - burger
  - Caribbean
  - seitan
  - jerk
  - fried chicken
servings: 4
---

-- Jamaican-spiced fibrous seitan chicken

Mix @vital wheat gluten{250%g}, @nutritional yeast{25%g}, @garlic powder{1 1/2%tsp}, @onion powder{1%tsp}, @sweet paprika{1%tsp}, @jerk seasoning{1 1/2%tsp}, @allspice{1/2%tsp}, @dried thyme{1/2%tsp}, @ground ginger{1/4%tsp}, @black pepper{1/4%tsp} and @salt{1/2%tsp} thoroughly.

Whisk together @cold vegetable stock{170%g}, @light soy sauce{25%g}, @neutral oil{15%g}, @tahini{10%g} and @liquid smoke{1/2%tsp} -- optional.

Pour the wet ingredients into the dry ingredients and mix until a dough forms.

Knead for ~{2%minutes} to ~{3%minutes} only. You want the gluten developed enough to hold together, but don't turn it into an extremely dense rubbery seitan.

Rest for ~{10%minutes}.

Pull the dough apart into irregular strips rather than simply forming four patties. Take a handful of strips and lay them roughly parallel, then compress them together into a flattened piece. Don't completely homogenise it; you want visible layers and strands. Make four pieces roughly the size of your burger buns.

Wrap each piece loosely in #baking parchment{} and then #foil, forming a flattened chicken-filleted shape.

Steam in a #steamer for ~{30%minutes}.

Remove and allow to cool completely.

Refrigerate for at least ~{1%hour}, preferably overnight. Chilling the steamed seitan makes it considerably easier to bread without it falling apart.

-- Jerk marinade

Whisk together @unsweetened plant milk{100%ml}, @lime juice{1%tbsp}, @hot sauce{1%tbsp}, @soy sauce{1%tbsp}, @jerk seasoning{1 1/2%tsp}, @allspice{1/2%tsp}, @garlic powder{1/2%tsp}, @onion powder{1/2%tsp}, @dried thyme{1/4%tsp}, @ground ginger{1/4%tsp} and @cayenne{1/16%tsp}.

Place the chilled seitan pieces in the marinade and leave for ~{20%minutes} to ~{30%minutes}. Don't leave them overnight; they'll absorb too much liquid and become harder to bread.

-- Crispy fried coating

Mix @plain flour{120%g}, @cornflour{60%g}, @baking powder{1%tsp}, @garlic powder{1%tsp}, @onion powder{1%tsp}, @sweet paprika{1%tsp}, @jerk seasoning{1/2%tsp}, @allspice{1/4%tsp}, @black pepper{1/2%tsp}, @white pepper{1/2%tsp}, @salt{1/2%tsp} and @cayenne{1/4%tsp} in a wide bowl.

In another bowl, mix @unsweetened plant milk{120%ml}, @hot sauce{1%tsp} and @jerk marinade{1%tsp}.

Take a marinated seitan piece and dredge it dry, then wet, then dry.

On the second dry coating, really press the flour mixture into the surface. This creates the craggy pieces of batter that give you a proper fried-chicken exterior.

For an even more aggressive crust, flick @wet coating mixture{1%tbsp} to @wet coating mixture{2%tbsp} into the dry flour and rub it through with your fingers before coating. This creates little clumps of seasoned flour which become crispy nuggets on the outside.

-- Frying

Heat @neutral frying oil{} in a #frying pan{} to 170–175°C.

Fry the seitan pieces individually for ~{3%minutes} to ~{4%minutes} per side, depending on thickness. Look for a deep golden-brown crust, a very crisp exterior and a hot centre.

Transfer the fried pieces to a #wire rack{} rather than kitchen paper.

Season immediately with @salt{1/16%tsp}.

Don't put the fried pieces directly onto a plate; the steam trapped underneath will soften the crust. A wire rack keeps the bottom crisp.

-- Mild jerk mayo

Mix @vegan mayonnaise{120%g}, @jerk seasoning{1 1/2%tsp}, @lime juice{1%tsp}, @maple syrup{1%tsp}, @light soy sauce{1/2%tsp}, @allspice{1/4%tsp}, @garlic powder{1/4%tsp}, @cayenne{1/16%tsp} and @hot sauce{1/2%tsp}.

Refrigerate for at least ~{30%minutes} before serving.

Taste before assembling. You want creaminess, gentle jerk spice, slight sweetness and lime.

If it tastes too spicy, add more @vegan mayonnaise{} and a tiny amount of @maple syrup{}.

-- Jerk BBQ sauce

Mix @vegan BBQ sauce{100%g}, @jerk seasoning{1%tsp}, @lime juice{1/2%tsp}, @brown sugar{1/2%tsp}, @allspice{1/4%tsp} and @cayenne{1/16%tsp} in a small #saucepan{}.

Warm gently for ~{2%minutes}, stirring until completely combined.

Taste and adjust. You want a sweet, smoky BBQ flavour with a subtle Jamaican spice character rather than a strongly jerk-flavoured sauce.

Allow to cool before serving.

-- Lime-smashed avocado

Mash @ripe avocados{2} roughly with @lime juice{15%ml} and @salt{1/4%tsp}. Don't blend it; you want small pieces of avocado remaining so it feels substantial in the burger.

-- Pink onions

Thinly slice @red onion{1}.

Mix @white vinegar{60%ml}, @water{60%ml}, @sugar{1%tbsp} and @salt{1/2%tsp}, then pour over the sliced onion.

Leave for at least ~{30%minutes}, preferably ~{1%hour}.

Drain thoroughly before putting the onions in the burger.

-- Wild rocket

Wash and thoroughly dry @wild rocket{60%g}.

Keep refrigerated until assembly.

-- Burger buns

Use @soft brioche-style vegan buns{4}.

Toast the cut sides in a #frying pan{} with a tiny amount of @vegan butter{}. You want them golden on the inside and soft everywhere else. Don't toast them until crunchy; the bun needs to compress around the fried seitan.

-- Assembly

Spread @jerk mayo{1%tbsp} over each bottom bun.

Add a small handful of @wild rocket{}.

Place one @crispy seitan chicken piece{} on top.

Add @smashed avocado{2%tbsp}.

Add @pink onions{1%tbsp} to @pink onions{2%tbsp}.

Drizzle @jerk BBQ sauce{1%tbsp} over the fried seitan.

Finish with @jerk mayo{1/2%tbsp} and place the top bun over the burger.

Give the top bun a gentle press. Don't squash it flat.

Serve immediately.

-- Restaurant finishing touch

For the best texture, assemble the burger immediately after frying the seitan. The contrast between the hot crispy coating, cool avocado, creamy jerk mayo, peppery rocket, sharp pink onions and sweet BBQ sauce is central to the burger.

Serve with @skin-on fries{} tossed with @salt{} immediately after frying.

-- Prep-ahead workflow

On day 1, make and steam the seitan, then chill overnight. Make the jerk mayo, jerk BBQ sauce and pink onions.

On day 2, marinate the seitan, bread to order, fry, toast the bun, smash the avocado, dress the rocket and assemble.

The seitan, sauces and onions can all be prepared ahead, meaning the actual burger takes only ~{5%minutes} to ~{7%minutes} to finish.

-- Key points

Don't compromise on @cornflour{} in the coating; it gives you that brittle crunch.

Don't compromise on the @jerk mayo{} and @BBQ sauce{} combination; Turtle Bay's own description identifies both as components of the Motherclucker.

Season the @seitan{} itself with Jamaican/jerk spices; Turtle Bay describes the patty as a chicken-style vegan patty "in Jamaican spices".

Use @wild rocket{} rather than ordinary lettuce for the peppery flavour.

Use @pink onions{} rather than cucumber pickles for the sharp, acidic element.

Don't compromise on chilled, fibrous seitan; don't fry freshly steamed seitan because the texture won't be nearly as convincing.
